WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:06.341
A useful alert tells the right reviewer about an event that may require a defined action.

00:00:06.341 --> 00:00:11.801
It should include camera, time, zone, event type, representative image or clip, model version,

00:00:11.801 --> 00:00:15.702
confidence signal, and enough preceding and following context for confirmation.

00:00:15.702 --> 00:00:18.432
Thresholds should reflect consequence and local conditions.

00:00:18.432 --> 00:00:22.723
A lower threshold may catch more events while increasing false positives.

00:00:22.723 --> 00:00:25.063
Schedules, regions of interest, direction, duration,

00:00:25.063 --> 00:00:30.133
and object size can make alerts more relevant without pretending uncertainty is gone.

00:00:30.133 --> 00:00:33.644
Group repeated frames and related detections into one event.

00:00:33.644 --> 00:00:39.494
Cooldown periods and escalation rules prevent a single ongoing condition from generating dozens of notifications.

00:00:39.494 --> 00:00:45.345
Route routine review to a queue and reserve immediate alerts for cases with a clear,

00:00:45.345 --> 00:00:46.125
time-sensitive response.

00:00:46.125 --> 00:00:48.856
Human review is essential before meaningful action.

00:00:48.856 --> 00:00:53.536
Reviewers should be able to confirm, dismiss, defer, add context, and escalate.

00:00:53.536 --> 00:00:56.656
When confidence is low or footage is unclear,

00:00:56.656 --> 00:01:00.947
the system should communicate uncertainty rather than automatically assume the worst.

00:01:00.947 --> 00:01:02.897
Document review decisions and outcomes.

00:01:02.897 --> 00:01:06.798
Feedback reveals confusing camera angles, recurring harmless activity, model drift,

00:01:06.798 --> 00:01:08.748
and rules that need tuning.

00:01:08.748 --> 00:01:13.428
Access to review queues and footage should follow role and privacy policy.

00:01:13.428 --> 00:01:18.109
Measure alert quality through confirmed relevance, missed events, review time, duplicate rate,

00:01:18.109 --> 00:01:20.059
escalation outcome, and reviewer feedback.

00:01:20.059 --> 00:01:22.789
Alerting is successful when it supports timely,

00:01:22.789 --> 00:01:27.860
accountable judgment without overwhelming people or turning uncertain model output into automatic accusation.
